[Music]  in our special series school matters  we're looking at how this pandemic is  affecting students all around the world  millions as you know have been forced to  switch to online classes which have  created you could say  a whole lot of new challenges now one  high school senior is hoping to make the  school year just a little bit easier  meg oliver introduces us to sophia jaffe  she's helping kids of all ages in a very  unique way  a smoothie that i made and i have my  mask  and my backpack and i am ready to go  seventeen-year-old sophia jaffe is back  to learning in person at her high school  in toronto  good morning she calls it a privilege to  be physically in class  all the desks are socially distant and  this is  what our notes look like on the board  sophia like millions of other students  had to turn her bedroom into a classroom  back in march when the coveted pandemic  began  how did that make you feel that social  isolation  it made me really upset actually because  this  part of high school where you get to  flourish it's really difficult to have  that  being taken away from you and then there  was the actual learning  when sophia went to seek out academic  help online she was surprised with what  she found  i remember thinking that you know the  school systems must have great lists of  recommended resources for online  learning  and i was wrong i was binge watching  netflix while doing school of course  and i did some research on netflix and i  saw that they personalized their  recommendations based on more than 75  000 categories  if we could take that level of  personalization and technology to online  learning  it could truly transform the world so  this summer sofia launched her own  website  elearn.fyi a collection of more than 300  online resources  it's one-stop shopping with links to  everything from coding and museum tours  to foreign languages  the free site has attracted visitors  from canada  the us and more than 100 other countries  i was so excited i sent it to all my  friends  and to the teachers and i even asked my  principal at the elementary school  to make sure like to send it to her her  staff because  it is useful karen dancy's 14 year old  son  liam recently started using the site for  help in math  i have a lot more questions it's hard to  understand things like through a  computer screen but with these websites  i can ask the questions  at any time i want really and i get and  i get the answers and i  succeed and i learn and understand  things well it's been a rocky time  you know nobody expected this  researchers found remote instruction  during this pandemic could put students  anywhere from three to 14  months behind in school robin lake is  the director of the university of  washington center on reinventing public  education  there's a lot out there it's just not  organized  and it's not available in a way to  parents and educators that helps them  separate  what's quality from what's not worth  their time  walking back to class  and that's exactly what sophia is trying  to change  she's helping put order to these chaotic  times  how does it feel knowing that you are  changing the landscape of online  learning  with all the uncertainty of online  learning  it's really difficult to digest and i  know that it  i can't be the only one feeling that way  a feeling turned into action  in a time of need for cbs this morning  meg oliver  new york we've got two customers with  liam and his mom i love liam sitting  there mom was just as excited  as everybody else when we were talking  about so what did she do exactly she  took  everything like one-stop shopping yeah  yeah  i know we're like you know please  translate well yeah  but you know it all began because she  was doing an online course  yeah while watching netflix which did  not escape the attention of parents and  teachers out there it's like that's  going on a lot yeah it is  yeah so you go to one site and then you  can get whatever you need yeah and  that's what i mean  there's a reason the mothers and are as  excited as the kids because  because this has been such a challenge  for parents yeah your wife's a teacher  yeah  and it's just it's really hard getting  everybody focused on the same page and  when kids fall behind  it's very hard to bring them back right  now so bravo sophia jeffy well done

